2 CONT -------------
2 CONT Obituary transcribed by Dena Gray-Fisher, source: Roland Record, April 19, 1923
2 CONT
2 CONT A Pioneer Resident Laid to Rest Last Saturday
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the earliest settlers in this community and one of the founders of the Bergen Lutheran church, Mr. Oscar (Aslak) Thompson was laid to rest last Saturday afternoon. Funeral services were held at the home of his daughter, Mrs
2 CONC . John Nereim, living north of town and from Bergen church at 3 o'clock. Rev. M. O. Sumstad spoke the words of comfort to the bereaved family. Interment took place at the Roland Cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Thompson was born at Nerstrad Prestagjeld, Norway, on May 4, 1835, and died at the home of his daughter, Mrs. Henry Stole, living near Forest City, on Wednesday evening, April 11, thereby having reached almost the age of 88 ye
2 CONC ars. He immigrated to this country in the year 1858 and came to Lisbon, Ill., and on November 4, the same year, he was united in holy marriage to Ragnhild Jacobson Aabo. He moved westward in the year of 1865 in covered prairie sch
2 CONC ooners with a band of other settlers, and established himself with his family on the old Thompson farm south of town. A few years ago he moved into town and has resided here since until about three years ago he was taken to Forest
2 CONC  City to make his home with Mr. and Mrs. Henry Stole.
2 CONT
2 CONT To this union nine children were born, of whom four preceded their father in death. Those surviving him are Mrs. John Nereim and Able Thompson of this place, Oscar O. Thompson of California, Mrs. John Halvorson of Dallas, Wis., an
2 CONC d Mrs. Henry Stole of Forest City, also 36 grandchildren and 21 great grandchildren. Mrs. Oscar Thompson passed away on December 4, 1922, at their home in south Roland. This pioneer couple were enthusiastic workers for church and 
2 CONC community and helpful where help was needed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Card of Thanks
2 CONT We wish to take this means of expressing our sincere thanks and appreciation to the many kind friends who expressed their sympathy to us in our recent bereavement in the loss of our beloved father, and for the beautiful floral gif
2 CONC ts. Especially do we thank Rev. Sumstad for his words of comfort.
2 CONT Mr. and Mrs. John Halvorson
2 CONT Mr. and Mrs. John Nereim
2 CONT Mr. and Mrs. Henry Stole
2 CONT Mr. and Mrs. A. B. Anderson
2 CONT Able Thompson
2 CONT Oscar Thompson
