2 CONT Obituary transcribed by Dena Gray-Fisher, source: Roland Record, June 1, 1944
2 CONT
2 CONT MRS. JOHN NEREM FUNERAL
2 CONT Funeral services were held Thursday afternoon of last week for Mrs. John Nerem, who passed away at her home in the southwest part of Roland following an illness of several years, the past year being bedfast. A short prayer service
2 CONC  was held at the home at 1:30 and service in Bergen Lutheran church at 2 o'clock, with Rev. Allen E. Nelson in charge. Special music was furnished by a group of ladies, and a solo by Otto J. Hanson. The pall bearers were Amos Thom
2 CONC pson, T. C. Jacobson, Gaylord Ness, Henry Birkeland, Ervin Anderson and Otis G. Sevde. Interment was made in the Roland cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Nerem nee Alice Thompson was born at Lisbon, Ill., on May 28, 1861, her parents being Aslak and Ragnhild Thompson. She can to Iowa with her parents at the age of three years and the family settled on a farm south of Roland wh
2 CONC ere she grew to womanhood.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was united in marriage to John Nerem on April 16, 1896, and they have made their home in and near Roland ever since. She is survived by two sons, Edward and Alfred Nerem, and one daughter, Mrs. Ruth Amundson of Ames, besides 1
2 CONC 4 grandchildren and three great grandchildren. She is also survived by one brother, Oscar Thompson of California.
2 CONT
2 CONT Among those from a distance in attendance at the funeral were Mr. and Mrs. Bob Hostetler, Ruth and Dickie of Colo, Mr. and Mrs. Herbert Fausch of Nevada, Mr. and Mrs. Channing McCahill, Mrs. Fern Moklebust and Kay, of Ames.
